6. Why separate Catalog and Inventory?

            This is a very important design question.
            
            Suppose we have:
                              Product:
                                  iPhone 17
                                  price = ₹80,000
                                  description = ...
                                  brand = Apple
            This is catalog information.

            But:
                  Available quantity = 27

                  is inventory information.

These behave very differently.

      Catalog            
            Usually:            
                        Read-heavy
                        Relatively stable
                        Searchable
                        Can be cached heavily
            
      Inventory                  
            Usually:      
                        Highly dynamic
                        Concurrency-sensitive
                        Correctness-sensitive
                        Frequent updates

If we put them together:        Product DB
                                       |
                                       ├── description
                                       ├── images
                                       ├── price
                                       └── inventory

then heavy product reads can interfere with inventory operations.


More importantly, the consistency requirements are different.

      For example:
                  It is usually acceptable if a product description takes a few seconds to propagate.

      But:
            Selling the same last item to two customers is a correctness problem.

This distinction will later influence our:
                              database choice
                              caching strategy
                              consistency model
                              scaling strategy
                              service boundaries

This is exactly the kind of reasoning we want to practice.
